---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Lay Servant Ministries — Reactivating the Basic Course"
  description="A pastor-team-taught, ten-hour hybrid Basic Course reactivating Lay Servant Ministries — leadership, preaching, and new faith communities led by laity, not just clergy."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <span class="status-tag eyebrow">In development · not yet scheduled</span>
    <h1>Not every call needs a seminary.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Lay Servant Ministries is a certification pipeline the Book of Discipline
      still fully provides for. In our conference, it currently sits dormant —
      no classes offered anywhere. This is a proposal, and the beginning of a
      build, to bring it back: a ten-hour hybrid Basic Course, taught by a
      small team of pastors, for laypeople who want to lead, preach, and care —
      without becoming clergy to do it.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/course">See the course</a>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="mailto:wilson.pruitt@gmail.com">Talk to Wilson about it</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <span class="eyebrow">Why now</span>
    <h2>The oldest Methodist pattern is a lay one.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The Discipline describes something much larger than pulpit supply:
        laity trained to lead, care, communicate, teach, and
        "develop new and existing faith communities" (¶268.1). In early
        Methodism, this was the engine of the movement — class leaders and
        lay preachers built the societies while ordained clergy remained few.
      </p>
      <p>
        The economics point the same direction. A new faith community that
        requires full clergy compensation is financially out of reach for
        most settings. The Discipline itself anticipates lay-led
        alternatives: a certified lay minister "is not eligible for support
        by equitable compensation funds or pension funds that are provided
        for clergy" (¶268.6). Reviving lay servant training is not just
        leadership development — it's the on-ramp for creative, low-cost new
        ministry.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="editorial-card">
      <p>
        A certified lay minister "is called and equipped to conduct public
        worship, care for the congregation, assist in program leadership,
        develop new and existing faith communities, preach the Word" — the
        Book of Discipline, ¶268.1
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <span class="eyebrow">The three-rung ladder</span>
    <h2>What the Discipline requires (¶¶266–268)</h2>
    <p class="lede">Each rung builds on the last — and each is a complete calling on its own.</p>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="rung-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">¶266</span>
        <h3>Certified Lay Servant</h3>
        <p>
          Basic course plus one advanced course, pastor and church council
          recommendation, district committee review. Leadership, teaching,
          caring ministry, and worship assistance in and beyond the local
          church.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rung-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">¶267</span>
        <h3>Certified Lay Speaker</h3>
        <p>
          Lay servant plus a track of study in leading worship, prayer, and
          preaching, district interview, and conference approval. The
          certification behind most pulpit supply.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rung-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">¶268</span>
        <h3>Certified Lay Minister</h3>
        <p>
          Public worship, congregational care, and developing new and
          existing faith communities — assigned by the district
          superintendent, outside the clergy compensation and pension
          structure.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <span class="eyebrow">The proposal</span>
    <h2>Ten hours, hybrid, five sessions, four pastors.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Two in-person Saturday bookends around three Zoom evenings, across
        five weeks — matching the official participant's book session for
        session. No pastor carries more than three to four hours of teaching
        time. Each pastor recruits three to five laity from their own
        charge, so a single cohort is connectional by design rather than
        one congregation's project.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top: var(--space-5);">
      <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/course">Read the full session-by-session plan →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <span class="eyebrow">Where this stands</span>
    <h2>Nothing is scheduled yet.</h2>
    <div class="status-panel">
      <dl>
        <dt>Dates</dt>
        <dd>Not set — waiting on a teaching team and a host church.</dd>
        <dt>Faculty</dt>
        <dd>Recruiting 2–3 pastor colleagues to teach one session each.</dd>
        <dt>Certifying structure</dt>
        <dd>Confirming with the district superintendent and conference lay leader who currently constitutes the district committee on Lay Servant Ministries (¶668.3), so completions count toward certification.</dd>
        <dt>Materials</dt>
        <dd><em>Lay Servant Ministries Basic Course Participant's Book</em> (Jackson &amp; Jackson, Discipleship Resources) — the official curriculum; this project doesn't alter it, only supplements it.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
    <p class="prose" style="margin-top: var(--space-5);">
      If you're a pastor in Rio Texas interested in teaching a session, or a
      layperson who wants to be first in line when a cohort forms, get in
      touch.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="mailto:wilson.pruitt@gmail.com">Email Wilson Pruitt</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
